<!-- Please read our Rules of Conduct: https://opensource.microsoft.com/codeofconduct/ -->
<!-- 🕮 Read our guide about submitting issues: https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/wiki/Submitting-Bugs-and-Suggestions -->
<!-- 🔎 Search existing issues to avoid creating duplicates. -->
<!-- 🧪 Test using the latest Insiders build to see if your issue has already been fixed: https://code.visualstudio.com/insiders/ -->
<!-- 💡 Instead of creating your report here, use 'Report Issue' from the 'Help' menu in VS Code to pre-fill useful information. -->
<!-- 🔧 Launch with `code --disable-extensions` to check. -->
Does this issue occur when all extensions are disabled?: Not checked in a normal workbench window. The automated reproduction isolates the built-in implementation with controlled service failures.

<!-- 🪓 If you answered No above, use 'Help: Start Extension Bisect' from Command Palette to try to identify the cause. -->
<!-- 📣 Issues caused by an extension need to be reported directly to the extension publisher. The 'Help > Report Issue' dialog can assist with this. -->
- VS Code Version: 1.143.0, affected build `087f03b7b82a0c89753986603421c3d24a24cd41`. The relevant implementation is unchanged on main at `5170ebe909381b6990cd686dc47da9c854bc5946`.
- OS Version: Windows x64; reproduced with the Electron unit-test runner.

Steps to Reproduce:

1. In `src/vs/sessions/contrib/chat/test/browser/sessionArtifacts.test.ts`, use the existing `createPresentation` fixture with a commit reference and a `getCommit` implementation that consistently rejects with `GitHubRequestError` of kind `authorization`/HTTP 403 or `notFound`/HTTP 404.
2. Observe `presentation.referenceSections` using `autorun` and allow the initial lookup to fail.
3. Update the artifact's presentation 20 times, preserving its ID, repository, commit hash, and link. Await each failure before the next update.
4. Count requests for that unchanged commit target.

### Actual

Both persistent HTTP 403 and HTTP 404 cases produce **21 lookup attempts for one commit**: the initial request plus one request for every artifact update. There is no failure cooldown, backoff, or negative-cache interval.

The reproduction used:

```ts
let attempts = 0;
const { presentation, artifacts } = createPresentation([artifact], undefined, undefined, async () => {
	attempts++;
	throw new GitHubRequestError('Controlled inaccessible resource', 'notFound', 404);
});
disposables.add(autorun(reader => {
	presentation.referenceSections.read(reader);
}));
await timeout(0);
for (let index = 0; index < 20; index++) {
	artifacts.set([{ ...artifact, label: `Recorded commit ${index}` }], undefined);
	await timeout(0);
}
assert.strictEqual(attempts, 21);
```

The same behavior was verified with `authorization`/403. `artifact` is an `ISessionArtifact` of kind `Commit` with a fixed GitHub commit URL.

### Expected

Presentation updates should not immediately retry the same persistently failing GitHub lookup. Apply failure-aware retry/backoff or negative caching, while permitting recovery when authentication/access or the resource identity changes, or when the user explicitly retries. Preserve successful metadata caching and cancellation/disposal behavior.

### Cause and Impact

[SessionGitHubCommitResolver.get](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/087f03b7b82a0c89753986603421c3d24a24cd41/src/vs/sessions/contrib/chat/browser/sessionArtifacts.ts#L476-L491) starts a request whenever `entry.store` is unset. On failure it disposes the request store and resets `currentEntry.store` to `undefined`, making the next presentation evaluation immediately eligible to request the same metadata again.

[The artifact sections derivation](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/blob/087f03b7b82a0c89753986603421c3d24a24cd41/src/vs/sessions/contrib/chat/browser/sessionArtifacts.ts#L638-L665) calls the resolver again when the artifact collection changes.

Successful lookups remain cached, whereas failures repeatedly consume network requests and log warnings. This can disproportionately inflate request-weighted failure rates and waste API quota/network activity without improving the displayed metadata.

### Validation and Scope

- The controlled 403 and 404 characterization cases passed with the expected 21 attempts each.
- They were executed alongside the paired PR/review polling reproduction using:

  ```powershell
  .\scripts\test.bat --run src\vs\sessions\contrib\github\test\browser\githubModels.test.ts --run src\vs\sessions\contrib\chat\test\browser\sessionArtifacts.test.ts --grep 'failure telemetry investigation:' --reporter spec
  ```

  Result: **4 characterization tests passed**. These confirm the undesirable behavior; they are not a fix. The temporary investigation tests are not on main.
- No live repository or connection was modified, and no inaccessible account credentials were used.
- A missing commit in an accessible repository returned HTTP 422 in the read-only GitHub probe, which is distinct from this 403/404 reproduction. Do not assume every missing-resource error means an unpublished commit.
- This report establishes repeat amplification. It does not identify the exact inaccessible resource or permission failure in any production telemetry.
"}

